# Line-ending policy — minimal, and it exists for one concrete defect.
#
# `core.autocrlf = true` on the authoring machine. With no .gitattributes, a
# FRESH CHECKOUT materialises CRLF in the working tree. The QROS state parser
# requires an LF-only state file and refuses otherwise:
#
#     UNREADABLE  qros-state.yaml: CR byte present; the state file is LF-only
#
# That was reproduced on a real `git worktree` checkout of the seal-persistence
# revision: the committed BLOB was LF-only and QROS derived SEAL = VERIFIED from
# it, but the checked-out FILE carried 338 CR bytes and could not be read. The
# seal pointer was durable in the object store and unusable from a fresh clone.
#
# Only the state file needs this. Every other pinned artifact is compared by
# `sha256` of its GIT BLOB (see research/extensions/x01/x01_runner.py), which is
# EOL-normalised by git and therefore platform-independent already; the parquet
# panels and the ETF CSV are git-ignored and pinned by raw-file sha256. So the
# rule is deliberately narrow rather than a repo-wide `* text=auto eol=lf`,
# which would renormalise unrelated files on the next checkout.
qros-state.yaml text eol=lf

# Same root cause, wider blast radius than the state file alone.
# `validate_wave0.py` pins its inputs by RAW working-tree sha256, which is not
# EOL-stable. Reproduced on a fresh worktree of the runner-base revision: 19 of
# its checks failed purely because .md/.csv materialised CRLF while the pins
# were computed from LF bytes (e.g. the sealed preregistration read
# d5ef963f... instead of 9c7b104f...). The validator's own logic is NOT touched
# — the fix is to make the checkout deterministic.
#
# Still deliberately narrow: named text types, not `* text=auto eol=lf`, which
# would sweep in unrelated binaries. These files are already LF in the working
# tree, so this changes nothing here and only makes fresh checkouts agree.
*.md   text eol=lf
*.csv  text eol=lf
*.py   text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
*.yml  text eol=lf

# The three IMMUTABLE Time-Series Value run artifacts are the one exception to
# `*.json text eol=lf` above, and the exception is deliberate.
#
# Their authoritative SHA256s were computed from the bytes the real S3 run
# actually produced and that Aaron accepted, and those bytes carry CRLF (the
# emitter wrote them with platform line endings). Those hashes are cited by the
# final Owner verdict in PROJECT_STATE.md, by the provenance correction sidecar,
# and by the knowledge-base cards. Under `text eol=lf` git stored an LF-
# normalised blob, so the committed bytes were NOT the hashed bytes and a fresh
# checkout could not reproduce them without the right local core.autocrlf.
#
# `-text` disables all EOL conversion for exactly these paths, so the blob holds
# the authoritative bytes verbatim and any checkout materialises them verbatim,
# independent of the user's core.autocrlf. The files themselves are NOT edited:
# re-indexing changes how git stores them, never their contents. Normalising
# them instead would have silently invalidated every recorded hash.
#
# Scoped to these three paths only. The repo-wide JSON policy is unchanged.
research/extensions/value/VALUE_EVIDENCE.json -text
research/extensions/value/VALUE_EVIDENCE_PROVENANCE_CORRECTION_001.json -text
research/extensions/value/VALUE_S3_AUTHORIZATION.json -text

# Checksum sidecars. `F6_FINAL_INDEPENDENT_REVIEW_2026-09-21_ASTRA_01.md.sha256`
# is the first one committed here; the repository's own convention is manifest
# pinning, and that manifest entry remains the binding pin. But a sidecar whose
# entire job is checksum verification must not itself be EOL-rewritten on
# checkout: `git add` warned "LF will be replaced by CRLF the next time Git
# touches it" on exactly this file. Same narrow treatment as the named text
# types above, for the same reason.
*.sha256 text eol=lf
